In general there's a lot that needs to happen with regard to monster AI
in Gehennom specifically, but the problem with Gehennom is that it
removes the advantage the monsters have over the player; numbers.

This line of purple monsters, for example:  ..@..HHATTHATT

...is far more disturbing in an open room than in a hallway, and
Gehennom is nothing but hallways.  So you mop them up one at a time,
loot the pile, and move on.  That has to be addressed by opening up
Gehennom somewhat, not by just making harder and harder monsters.

> While I'm at it I wonder how hard it is to teach a monster to stop
> zapping a wand when it sees you have reflection.

Both trivial and slightly tricky.  The mon structure would need a huge
new set of flags for "things this monster knows about you"; fortunately,
adding this and then tacking on the rest of the logic is mostly a case
of logging the coding time required as opposed to any elaborate design.

It's a good idea, though, so I'll add it to my list of things to
possibly include in my upcoming big patch/release (which I'm holding off
on until after June, since most of the player mindshare).  Hopefully I
can get a bit more time to get things like this in to cover the most
basic, obvious cases... which shouldn't be too unbalancing.
(Shopkeepers would get smarter, for example, but newbies can't kill
shopkeepers straight up anyway so it doesn't hurt them.)

